# docker compose settings. Copy to .env:   cp .env.example .env
# The .env file is not tracked by Git (it holds passwords). Permissions on the server: chmod 600 .env
#
# For running on a Mac via ./scripts/start-all.sh the default values are enough.
# Lines starting with "#" are optional: the value shown is the one used by default.

# ============================================================================
# 1. BASICS (database and pgAdmin passwords are required; login passwords are required only on a server; Excel is optional)
# ============================================================================

# PostgreSQL password. On a server replace it with a random one: openssl rand -hex 24
# Letters and digits only: the password is substituted into the DATABASE_URL connection string.
# IMPORTANT: the password is written to the database on the FIRST start. If you change it in .env later, the database
# keeps the old password and the backend can no longer connect.
# Correct order for changing it: docs/DEPLOY.md, section 10.5.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=expenses

# pgAdmin login (web UI for the database). Replace the password with your own.
# Like the database password, it is read only on the first pgAdmin start (section 10.5a in docs/DEPLOY.md).
PGADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
PGADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# Passwords for logging in to the app, at least 8 characters. Generate: openssl rand -base64 18
# Required on a server (docker-compose.public.yml); not needed when running only the database for development.
# Logins: misha and nastya (the user list and their default cards: AUTH_USERS below).
# On every start the backend hashes these passwords (scrypt) and stores only the hash. To change a password,
# change the value here and restart the backend (docker compose up -d): all sessions of that user will end.
# The value in .env is plain text: keep the file at permissions 600 and do not publish it.
AUTH_PASSWORD_MISHA=
AUTH_PASSWORD_NASTYA=

# Path to the Excel file in OneDrive. OPTIONAL: if the variable is empty or unset, Excel is disabled and
# expenses are stored in the database only (the sync icon is not shown in the UI).
# If set but the file is missing or OneDrive is unavailable, the app does not crash: expenses are saved to the database
# and wait in a queue until the file becomes available.
# Use a test file first and switch to the real one after checking (docs/DEPLOY.md, section 10.6).
ONEDRIVE_FILE_PATH=Документы/Расходы-dev.xlsx
# Name of the remote in the rclone config (secrets/rclone.conf)
# ONEDRIVE_REMOTE=onedrive

# ============================================================================
# 2. EXCEL
# ============================================================================

# Sheet and structured table where expenses are written
# EXCEL_SHEET_NAME=Лист1
# EXCEL_TABLE_NAME=Операции

# ============================================================================
# 3. APPLICATION
# ============================================================================

# App users: login:default card, comma-separated. Login: lowercase Latin letters, digits, _.
# The card is preselected in the expense form for that user. For a new user add
# their password as the AUTH_PASSWORD_<LOGIN> variable (and a line in docker-compose.yml). Users removed from the list are disabled.
# AUTH_USERS=misha:Миша,nastya:Настя
# Who can edit and delete records on the "Операции" tab (logins, comma-separated); other users are read-only.
# Edits and deletions are carried over to Excel immediately: the row is replaced or removed (if the file is busy, the change waits in a queue).
# EDITOR_USERS=misha
# Application administrators (manage groups), comma-separated logins; none by default
# ADMIN_USERS=misha
# Name of the group created for the existing users on the first start
# DEFAULT_GROUP_NAME=Семья
# How long a user stays signed in without re-entering the password:
# the access token (login cookie) lives 24 hours, then is renewed via the refresh token (7 days, extended on use),
# but no longer than SESSION_MAX_DAYS from the moment the password was entered.
# ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=24
# REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=7
# SESSION_MAX_DAYS=30
# Minimum login password length (at least 8)
# AUTH_MIN_PASSWORD_LENGTH=8
# Additional site addresses from which requests are allowed (usually not needed: the site's own address is checked)
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Yerevan
# Base currency: bookkeeping and Excel are kept in it. Other currencies from CURRENCIES are converted to it at the rate
# DEFAULT_CURRENCY=AMD
# Currencies in which expenses can be entered (comma-separated list; each needs a rate in the source)
# CURRENCIES=AMD,RUB,USD,EUR
# Rate source: official rates of the Central Bank of Armenia (drams per unit of currency). The rate is fetched every few hours
# and stored in the database; if the source is unavailable, the last known rate is used, and a rate can be entered manually
# EXCHANGE_RATES_URL=https://cb.am/latest.json.php
# EXCHANGE_RATES_REFRESH_HOURS=6
# Comma-separated lists. Values from the database (including from Excel) are added to the categories.
# CARDS=Миша,Настя
# CATEGORIES=кафе,одежда,продукты,жилье,маркетплейс,другое
# How many days to keep Excel backups and duplicate-protection keys
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ============================================================================
# 4. EXCEL SYNC
# ============================================================================

# How often to reconcile Excel and the database
# SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5
# While records cannot be appended to Excel (file is open), retry more often
# SYNC_RETRY_SECONDS=60
# How many seconds to wait for the Excel write before responding to expense creation (longer: the expense stays queued)
# SYNC_INLINE_WAIT_SECONDS=15
# Mass-loss protection: what share of records may be marked deleted in one cycle
# (if more disappear, e.g. when Excel is replaced by an empty file, the deletion is not applied)
# SYNC_MAX_DELETE_PERCENT=25

# ============================================================================
# 5. PORTS (all listen on 127.0.0.1 only)
# ============================================================================

# Web UI: http://127.0.0.1:8080
# HTTP_PORT=8080
# HTTPS_PORT=8443
# pgAdmin: http://127.0.0.1:5051
# PGADMIN_PORT=5051
# PostgreSQL on localhost (to run the backend outside Docker). Not 5432, to avoid interfering with other databases.
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# Address the web service listens on. 0.0.0.0 opens the port to everyone: only for public access from section 6,
# where the site is password-protected.
# WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1

# ============================================================================
# 6. PUBLIC INTERNET ACCESS (docker-compose.public.yml): HTTPS
# ============================================================================
# Needed on a server only. Step-by-step guide: docs/DEPLOY.md, section 8.

# --- COMPOSE_FILE: which compose files `docker compose` reads ---------------
# This is a standard Docker Compose variable. It replaces the -f flags:
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.public.yml
# is equivalent to `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.public.yml ...`.
# Files are separated by a colon (on Windows by ";"); each next one extends and overrides
# the previous: docker-compose.public.yml opens ports 80/443, mounts Caddyfile.public and the password.
# Without this line a plain `docker compose up -d` starts only the base file, i.e. "localhost only" mode:
# the site disappears from outside, and on update (`git pull && docker compose up -d --build`) it is easy to miss that
# public mode has been switched off. If the line is not set, Docker Compose reads only docker-compose.yml.
# docker-compose.public.yml alone does not put a password on the site: app login always works.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.public.yml

# Site address. A domain (e.g. expenses.example.com) or a free address made from the server IP with dashes
# (for IP 1.2.3.4: 1-2-3-4.nip.io). Caddy obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate itself; for that, ports
# 80 and 443 must be open and the name must point to the server (check: dig +short your-address).
# SITE_ADDRESS=expenses.example.com
# Separate address for pgAdmin (a domain needs its own A record; for nip.io: pgadmin.1-2-3-4.nip.io)
# PGADMIN_ADDRESS=pgadmin.example.com

# Site login is protected by the app itself (logins and passwords above); there is no separate Caddy password on the site.
# The Caddy password (Basic Auth) is needed only for pgAdmin: login and password hash.
# PGADMIN_BASIC_AUTH_USER=admin
# The hash is created with this command (the password is typed at the prompt and stays out of history; needs a terminal):
#   docker run --rm -it expenses-tracker-web caddy hash-password
# The value MUST be in single quotes: the hash contains $ characters
# PGADMIN_BASIC_AUTH_HASH='$2a$14$...'
